WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.050
Narrator: The world produces
enough plastic each year

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.000
to build 50 Pyramids of Giza.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.510
That's over 350 million
tons of candy wrappers,

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.530
PVC pipes, and synthetic T-shirts.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.180
While most of it ends up in landfills,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.800
eight million tons of plastic winds up

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.150
in our oceans each year

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:23.570
where most of it finds its way

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.970
into massive garbage
patches around the world

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.350
and the biggest of them all

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.883
is called the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.

00:00:35.150 --> 00:00:37.730
If you picked up each piece of plastic

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.890
in the Great Pacific Garbage Patch,

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:44.890
you'd carry away about 1.8
trillion individual pieces.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.840
That's 10 times more plastic pieces

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:52.630
than there are stars
in our Milky Way Galaxy

00:00:52.630 --> 00:00:56.890
and it would weigh a whopping 80,000 tons

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.233
equivalent to the weight of
three Statues of Liberty.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.570
Half of the entire patch is made

00:01:03.570 --> 00:01:06.140
of plastic fishing nets, lines and ropes

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:09.780
which come from intense
fishing activity near the area.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:13.340
The other half is mostly
hard plastics and films

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.080
like water bottles and plastic wrap

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.180
but don't let the name Great
Pacific Garbage Patch fool you.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.850
It doesn't look like a giant
mountain of trash at all.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:26.760
It's actually scattered
over a region of ocean

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:31.330
that's twice the size of Texas
according to some estimates.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:34.649
So, if you wanted to pick
up every piece of plastic,

00:01:34.649 --> 00:01:38.890
it would take you 121
days at a walking pace

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.773
of five kilometers per hour
to cover the entire area.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.970
Though in reality there's no true end

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:48.260
since the garbage patch

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:52.420
is constantly ebbing and
flowing with the ocean currents.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.120
But let's pretend we could
scoop it all up into one place.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.120
There'd be enough plastic to
fill 100 Boeing 747 planes

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:05.560
and the patch is only getting bigger.

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.500
It's actually been growing
exponentially larger

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:11.320
for nearly 70 years.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.160
Partly because once the plastic is there,

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:17.030
it'll stick around for centuries.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:19.510
Those plastic fishing lines, for example,

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.050
will take 600 years to break down

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:24.960
and even after they break down,

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:27.284
the damage doesn't stop there.

00:02:27.284 --> 00:02:29.730
Most end up as microplastics

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.780
that are too small to
see with the naked eye

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:34.700
but can make it into the bellies

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:38.300
of sea animals and, ultimately, the humans

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:40.590
that will eat those animals.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:42.990
Worldwide, researchers have found

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:46.100
ingested microplastics in every species

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:50.140
of sea turtle, nearly 60% of whale species

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:52.443
and almost 60% of sea birds.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.500
Plus more plastic is pouring

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.090
into the world's oceans each day.

00:02:58.090 --> 00:03:02.550
In fact, experts estimate
that by the year 2050,

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:04.500
the amount of plastic in the oceans

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.313
will outweigh all the world's fish.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.070
Think about that the next time
you toss your water bottle

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.203
in the trash because the recycling's full.

